"""Model checking of the HCPN models generated by the exporter.

CPN Tools answers verification questions interactively: enter the state-space
tool, calculate the occurrence graph and the SCC graph, paste an ASK-CTL script
into an auxiliary box and evaluate it. This module answers the same questions
from Python, on the very ``.cpn`` file CPN Tools would open, so verification can
run on every refinement of every model. Its answers are cross-checked against
CPN Tools itself (see :mod:`pyruleanalyzer.cpntools_oracle`).

Method
------
Four techniques are used, and they are kept apart because they establish
different things:

``ctl``
    Explicit-state CTL model checking (Clarke, Grumberg, Kroening, Peled &
    Veith, *Model Checking*, 2nd ed., MIT Press, 2018; Baier & Katoen,
    *Principles of Model Checking*, MIT Press, 2008). The occurrence graph of
    the Coloured Petri Net is built by :mod:`pyruleanalyzer.cpn_statespace` --
    markings carry real token values, there is no abstraction -- and read as a
    Kripke structure ``K = (S, s0, R, L)``: ``S`` the reachable markings, ``R``
    the occurrences of binding elements, ``L`` the atomic propositions below.
    Formulas are evaluated with the standard fixpoint characterisations
    (``EF``/``E[U]`` as least fixpoints of backward reachability, ``AF`` as the
    least fixpoint of "every successor is in the set").

``scc``
    Home markings, from the decomposition of the occurrence graph into
    strongly connected components: a home marking exists exactly when there is
    a single terminal component (Jensen & Kristensen, *Coloured Petri Nets*,
    Springer, 2009). This is not a CTL formula, and it is what CPN Tools'
    ``ListHomeMarkings`` computes.

``structural``
    Static analysis of the leaf guards, which are conjunctions of axis-aligned
    bounds: two leaves overlap exactly when their boxes intersect. The answer
    holds for every input, which no single state space can show.

``testing``
    Conformance of the net with the classifier on a set of inputs. A
    functional-equivalence test on those inputs, not a temporal property.

Dead markings
-------------
Textbook CTL assumes a total transition relation. The generated nets are meant
to stop, so dead markings are part of the model, and the semantics has to say
what a path formula means when a path ends. Here a dead marking is given a
self-loop (Baier & Katoen, Sec. 3.1), which on the operators used makes a
finite maximal path a counterexample to ``AF phi`` if it ends before ``phi``.

CPN Tools' ASK-CTL does something else: its ``FORALL_UNTIL`` (and so ``EV``)
is *vacuously true* at a dead marking -- the "for all successors" of a node
without successors holds -- so ``EV(phi)`` means ``AF(phi or dead)``. A net that
deadlocks before predicting satisfies ``EV(PRED)``. The two readings differ only
for ``AF``-type properties and only when a dead marking without ``phi`` is
reachable; both are computed, and the ASK-CTL scripts generated here encode
``AF phi`` as ``EV(phi) AND NOT E[not phi U (dead and not phi)]`` so that CPN
Tools answers the textbook question.

Properties
----------
===== ============================== ==================================== ==========
ID    Property                       Formula                              Method
===== ============================== ==================================== ==========
A1    Termination                    AF dead                              ctl
A2    No spurious deadlock           !EF(dead & !pred)                    ctl
A3    Inevitable decision            AF pred                              ctl
A4    Prediction recoverability      AG EF pred                           ctl
A5    Unique output                  AG |Prediction| <= 1                 ctl
A6    Valid label                    AG Prediction subset L               ctl
A7    Safeness                       AG forall p: |p| <= 1                ctl
A8    Home marking                   one terminal SCC                     scc
B1    Leaf determinism               forall T: AG |EN(leaves_T)| <= 1     ctl
B2    Inevitable leaf selection      forall T: AF EN(leaves_T)            ctl
B3    Single tree output             forall T: AG |out_T| <= 1            ctl
B4    Guard disjointness             forall T, i!=j: box_i & box_j = {}   structural
C1    Stage precedence               forall k,m: !E[!acc_k,m-1 U acc_k,m] ctl
C2    No premature score             forall k: !E[!acc_k,M U score_k]     ctl
C3a   Inevitable decision firing     AF EN(decide)                        ctl
C3b   Decision determinism           AG |EN(decide)| <= 1                 ctl
D1    Complete vote                  AG(EN(vote) -> all votes present)    ctl
D2a   Inevitable aggregation         AF EN(vote)                          ctl
D2b   Single aggregation             AG |Prediction| <= 1                 ctl
PC    Prediction consistency         net(x) = model(x)                    testing
===== ============================== ==================================== ==========

``EN(G)`` holds in a marking with an outgoing arc whose transition is in ``G``;
``|EN(G)|`` counts those arcs. ``T`` ranges over tree subpages, ``k`` over GBDT
class channels, ``m`` over boosting stages.
"""

